#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128955 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129140 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40733109-8985-5f30-9678-289216c1da00" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "75d559bd-99c1-5374-9ae6-0096a8b1d86e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5806" :question/problem "Sketch the curve $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}(x-4)(x^2 + 5)$." :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of\n\n$$\nx^2 + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 0^2 - 4(1)(5) = -20 < 0\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{5}(0 - 4)(0^{2} + 5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}(-4)(5) \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-4)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4$, and\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8d12adf0f04b7c1d9d05271c9378b941cf107d713932055a1fc48371952a1c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128956 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "1ebf167f-0c61-5721-9019-1da86d591c7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5808" :question/problem "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 4x - 24$, sketch the curve $y = f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n$x^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}- 4\\begin{vmatrix}-24 \\\\ 3\\end{vmatrix}12\\begin{vmatrix}24 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid 8 \\mid 0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f(x)}{x-3} = x^2 + 4x + 8\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x^2 + 4x + 8)\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of\n\n$$\nx^2 + 4x + 8 = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 4^2 - 4(1)(8) = -16 < 0\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0^{3} + 0^{2} - 4(0) - 24 =-24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-24)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3$, and\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/fa/fa1af56b5acbb196567d5943db53cf34405ab59cdb5e73a8a9fc8021d2864373.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "acf8ac05-4080-50ea-8a33-dca4a08cb7e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5810" :question/problem "Sketch the curve $f(x) = -(x-2)(x^2 + 2x + 2)$." :question/worked-solution "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 2 = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 2^2 - 4(1)(2) = -4 < 0\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= - (0 - 2)(0^{2} + 2(0) + 2) \\\\\n&= - (-2)(2) \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2$, and\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/1b/1bbbfefc0260959a5008b5b4597293e0d4acdeb390601cfdb53a8d700ffbbf54.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129146 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "50dda1cf-e61a-5efb-9671-a66d71360b84" :question/math-academy-id "e-5813" :question/problem "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x) = -x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + 16$, sketch the curve $y=f(x)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\nx^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} & x^{1} & x^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}- 4\\begin{vmatrix}16 \\\\ -4\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}-16 \\\\ -1\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid - 4 \\mid 0\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f(x))/(x - 4) &= -x^{2} - 4 \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = -(x-4)(x^2 + 4)\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of\n\n$$\nx^2 + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0^2 - 4(1)(4) =-16 < 0\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4$.\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny = -(0)^3 + 4(0)^2 - 4(0) + 16 = 16\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,16)$,\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4$, and\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/aa/aa0418567de10fa6bd229a3d1bfbff2a71f242232f8af8dd001b096ecc9c8038.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129143 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b16f1085-6b45-5b90-8c6d-7f7be91cd536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/542195ea9617c2d674bb5a96a116033db8204114a5c8ba44a164c82b6133c552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126918} {:answer/id #uuid "10f104f2-cc54-5f5c-a53b-614166d0293b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/03/03871a7ce58953f8cca6239641e0b18b7a4fcaed50fbed781c097ce2acbc2f08.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126919} {:answer/id #uuid "119a15c5-2653-58d3-9dfe-83a5f6f95715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2f/2fe937dda01135a13db51ef5a3b5f924f0e53aab59e4fe583a2a55bac753d995.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126920} {:answer/id #uuid "17439721-eac2-5a3e-89b7-5307977ce0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126921} {:answer/id #uuid "1a1e691e-5f3e-549d-b797-a753fd439226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3df55302db431e7e2bc937028b1737956ae73299b59cf07be8e7b2fa02d2b87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126922}] :answer-field/id #uuid "69675294-a915-57a9-baa8-613457e1b1e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126917}] :question/id #uuid "4af252a3-6966-5006-8a08-9a500d4173b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-28196" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y = f(x)$ where $f(x) = \\frac{1}{3}(2 - x)(x^{2} + 3x + 3)$?\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129141 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dfd8d40-ee79-5c13-999a-273c61ccfd3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a11984f9a9de7e220103525a3c925dee83a9eed37c4901f46046de7e80bc3a7f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324337} {:answer/id #uuid "46e897b5-bae2-55bb-a504-26d099cf0345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/585b326719cd74e9a7b26892df49742083ccafeea83379a517552f78be83e5ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324542} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecac441-46f0-5eeb-a26a-0dff106b6759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/98705a867d25e4f8991c29e9cda964b694c678cb1cb2de638f4d1d2f1ae37200.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324617} {:answer/id #uuid "7da9e0e7-1b33-585d-8215-ce739e8fcb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325030} {:answer/id #uuid "93f8f1b0-6195-5f5a-a9e9-03cc49116f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/851b996ce1dab2e5777b5c7475785863009c0b8519b2805da72cbb4c07afcb47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7da9e0e7-1b33-585d-8215-ce739e8fcb45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325030} :answer-field/id #uuid "a692b341-ff58-5a69-9290-ce953c4bcc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f032169-ef22-5e6b-92e9-31b38bfaf554" :question/math-academy-id "q-39135" :question/problem "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)"}]}
